---
/**
 * One drawn figure per feature area, keyed by slug. THREE OF THE FIVE AREAS GET
 * ONE. The other two render nothing, and that is the decision rather than a gap:
 *
 *   the-day-itself        DECLINED. Its shape is the loop, and the loop is
 *                         already drawn twice - LoopBand in the hero and the
 *                         workflow rail. LoopBand's own comment says two
 *                         drawings of one chain that disagree are worse than no
 *                         drawing, and a third would be two chances to disagree.
 *   team-and-connections  DECLINED. Setup, roles, offline behaviour and two
 *                         unbuilt connectors are a list, not a shape. A figure
 *                         added so that all five pages match is the persona-grid
 *                         mistake drawn instead of written.
 *
 * A figure earns its place here when the ARGUMENT is geometric - when the shape
 * makes the point faster than the paragraph above it can. That is true of an
 * arithmetic path, of a gate, and of a split between what ships and what does
 * not. It is not true of a list.
 *
 * EVERY FIGURE'S GEOMETRY IS IN PIXELS, NOT REM. These are drawings; their
 * labels are text and still scale with the reader's font size, but bar heights,
 * gaps and marks do not, because at 200% text a rem-sized mark grows while its
 * column does not and ends up clipped inside a row that still reports zero page
 * overflow. That failure shipped once on the homepage figure. The probe is a
 * per-child collision and scrollWidth check, not scrollWidth on the document.
 *
 * THE GRID TRACKS ARE PIXELS FOR THE SAME REASON. A minmax(9rem, 1fr) column
 * floor is 288px once the reader doubles the root size, which is wider than a
 * 360px phone's content box, and the page scrolls sideways (WCAG 1.4.10). It
 * did exactly that here before these were pinned.
 *
 * NO NEW CLAIMS. Each figure redraws something the page above it already says
 * and the ledger already backs: RC-16 and RC-19 for the costing path, RC-09,
 * RC-38 and RC-39 for the intake gate, RC-42 for the nutrition split. If a
 * figure ever needs a sentence its area page does not already carry, it needs a
 * ledger row first.
 */
interface Props {
	slug: string;
}
const { slug } = Astro.props;

// The path a case price walks before it is a number you can say out loud. This
// is SECTION_META's own lede for the area, drawn: a yield, a sub-recipe, a
// portion size and a guest count.
const costingPath = [
	{ op: null, tag: 'CASE', label: 'What the supplier charged for the whole pack.' },
	{ op: 'divided by the pack', tag: 'UNIT', label: 'A price per kilo, per litre, per each.' },
	{ op: 'divided by the yield', tag: 'EDIBLE', label: 'What is left once the skins and the cores are gone.' },
	{ op: 'times the recipe line', tag: 'PLATE', label: 'The share of one dish that ingredient is.' },
	{ op: 'times the guest count', tag: 'EVENT', label: 'The food cost the quote gets judged against.' }
];

// Four doors, one queue, and a gate. The gate is the whole argument.
const intakeFlow = [
	{
		tag: 'FOUR DOORS',
		gate: false,
		label: 'A photograph, a PDF or a doc, a spreadsheet, pasted text. The queue does not care which it was.'
	},
	{
		tag: 'ONE QUEUE',
		gate: false,
		label: 'Read, and matched against the ingredients you already have. Nothing saved.'
	},
	{
		tag: 'YOU CONFIRM',
		gate: true,
		label: 'A list of differences to look at. Whatever could not be made out is quoted back as it appeared.'
	},
	{
		tag: 'YOUR PRICES',
		gate: false,
		label: 'Only the lines you said yes to. The rest waits, or gets keyed in by hand.'
	}
];

const nutrientCount = 15;
---

{
	slug === 'recipes-and-costing' && (
		<figure class="mt-12" data-reveal>
			<div class="border-y border-ticket-rule bg-paper px-5 py-8 sm:px-8">
				<p class="sr-only">
					The path a case price walks, in five steps. What the supplier charged for the
					whole pack, divided by the pack to give a price per unit, divided by the yield to
					give the price of the part you can actually serve, times the recipe line to give
					that ingredient's share of one dish, and times the guest count to give the food
					cost the quote is judged against.
				</p>
				<ol
					role="list"
					class="grid grid-cols-[repeat(auto-fit,minmax(144px,1fr))] gap-x-6 gap-y-8"
					aria-hidden="true"
				>
					{costingPath.map((step) => (
						<li class="min-w-0">
							{/* The operator sits ABOVE its stop rather than between two of
							    them, so it stays attached to the right stop however the grid
							    wraps. A glyph between columns points at the wrong neighbour
							    the moment a row breaks, which is the failure LoopBand
							    records for its arrows. */}
							<p class="h-4 font-mono text-eyebrow tracking-wide text-ink-soft">
								{step.op ?? ''}
							</p>
							<div class="mt-2 border-t-2 border-amber pt-3">
								<p class="font-mono text-eyebrow font-semibold tracking-wide text-amber-deep">
									{step.tag}
								</p>
								<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-snug text-ink-soft">{step.label}</p>
							</div>
						</li>
					))}
				</ol>
			</div>
			<figcaption class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-sm text-ink-soft">
				<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">One price, four steps.</strong>{' '}
				Every one of them stays on the screen, so a cost you do not believe can be taken
				apart until you find the step you disagree with.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>
	)
}

{
	slug === 'getting-prices-in' && (
		<figure class="mt-12" data-reveal>
			<div class="border-y border-ticket-rule bg-paper px-5 py-8 sm:px-8">
				<p class="sr-only">
					How a price gets in, in four steps. Four doors: a photograph, a PDF or a
					document, a spreadsheet, or pasted text. One queue, where it is read and matched
					against the ingredients you already have, with nothing saved. Then you confirm:
					a list of differences to look at, with whatever could not be made out quoted
					back as it appeared. Only then, your prices, and only the lines you said yes to.
					The rest waits, or gets keyed in by hand.
				</p>
				<ol
					role="list"
					class="grid grid-cols-[repeat(auto-fit,minmax(160px,1fr))] gap-x-6 gap-y-8"
					aria-hidden="true"
				>
					{intakeFlow.map((step) => (
						<li class="min-w-0">
							{/* The gate is the only stop drawn differently, because it is the
							    only stop that is a promise rather than a mechanism. Amber is
							    the warning colour in this system and that is the correct
							    register: it marks the step where a human has to act. */}
							<div
								class:list={[
									'border-t-2 pt-3',
									step.gate ? 'border-amber' : 'border-ink-rule/45'
								]}
							>
								<p
									class:list={[
										'font-mono text-eyebrow font-semibold tracking-wide',
										step.gate ? 'text-amber-deep' : 'text-ink-soft'
									]}
								>
									{step.tag}
								</p>
								<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-snug text-ink-soft">{step.label}</p>
							</div>
						</li>
					))}
				</ol>
			</div>
			<figcaption class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-sm text-ink-soft">
				<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">The gate in the middle is the point.</strong>{' '}
				Everything to the left of it is a proposal. Reading the paper is the fast part;
				saying yes to it stays yours.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>
	)
}

{
	slug === 'compliance-and-labels' && (
		<figure class="mt-12" data-reveal>
			<div class="border-y border-ticket-rule bg-paper px-5 py-8 sm:px-8">
				<p class="sr-only">
					Two halves, drawn side by side. On the left, the fifteen nutrients an FDA panel
					carries, all fifteen computed per recipe from USDA profiles. On the right, the
					printed sheet, drawn as a filled panel because it prints from the browser today,
					and beside it a label printer drawn as an empty outline, because the kitchen
					labels print through the browser too and no direct printer connection is built.
				</p>
				<div class="grid gap-10 sm:grid-cols-2 sm:gap-12" aria-hidden="true">
					<div class="min-w-0">
						<p class="font-mono text-eyebrow font-semibold tracking-wide text-ink-soft">
							THE NUMBERS
						</p>
						{/* Fifteen marks at a fixed pixel size. They are a count, not text, so
						    they must not grow with the reader's font size. */}
						<div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap gap-[6px]">
							{Array.from({ length: nutrientCount }).map(() => (
								<span class="block size-[14px] bg-softgreen ring-1 ring-ink-rule/45" />
							))}
						</div>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-[34ch] text-sm leading-snug text-ink-soft">
							All fifteen an FDA panel carries, computed per recipe from USDA profiles. It
							says so when a profile is missing rather than totalling an incomplete dish.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="min-w-0">
						<p class="font-mono text-eyebrow font-semibold tracking-wide text-amber-deep">
							THE PAPER, AND THE PRINTER
						</p>
						<div class="mt-5 flex items-end gap-3">
							<div class="h-[64px] w-[112px] border-2 border-ink-rule/60 bg-paper" />
							<div class="h-[40px] w-[64px] border-2 border-dashed border-amber bg-softamber/40" />
						</div>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-[34ch] text-sm leading-snug text-ink-soft">
							The sheet and the kitchen date labels print from the browser today. No direct
							connection to a label printer is built, so the printer stays an outline.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<figcaption class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-sm text-ink-soft">
				<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">The numbers ship, and so does the paper. The browser does the printing.</strong>{' '}
				Drawing the difference is more honest than a badge, because a badge is the thing a
				reader skips.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>
	)
}
